Alternatively, maybe the race is still 60 meters, but Petya starts 9 meters behind the starting line, so he has to run 60 meters, but Vasya starts 9 meters ahead? Wait, the problem says "Petya started exactly 9 meters behind Vasya." So if they are both running the same race, but Petya starts 9 meters behind. So, if Vasya is at the starting line, then Petya is at -9 meters? So when they start running, Petya has to run 60 + 9 = 69 meters to finish, while Vasya runs 60 meters. That seems to make sense. So the second race is a 60-meter race, but Petya starts 9 meters behind. Therefore, the total distance Petya has to run is 69 meters, and Vasya has to run 60 meters.

But I need to make sure. Maybe the second race is also a 60-meter race, but they start at different positions. But the problem doesn't specify the length of the second race, so I think the standard is that the race is 60 meters, but Petya starts 9 meters behind. So, in the second race, Petya's race is 69 meters, while Vasya's race is 60 meters.

